                "content": "{\n  \"solution\": \"### Summary ###\\n\\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\n**b. Method Sketch:**\\n1. By permuting rows and columns we may assume that the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares $(i,i)$, $1\\\\le i\\\\le n$ (here $n=2025$).  This does not change the number of tiles needed.\\n2. A concrete tiling with $2n-2$ tiles is given: for each column $j$ with $1\\\\le j\\\\le n-1$ place a vertical $ (n-j)\\\\times1$ tile covering rows $j+1,\\\\dots,n$; for each column $j$ with $2\\\\le j\\\\le n$ place a vertical $ (j-1)\\\\times1$ tile covering rows $1,\\\\dots,j-1$.  The $2n-2$ tiles cover every unit square except the diagonal.\\n3. A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles holds for any tiling of the board with diagonal holes.  Any rectangle that avoids the diagonal must lie entirely either strictly above it (all its cells satisfy row $<$ column) or strictly below it (row $>$ column).  In the upper‑triangular part the $n-1$ cells $(i,i+1)$, $1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1$, are pairwise such that a single rectangle can contain at most one of them; therefore at least $n-1$ rectangles are needed to cover the upper part.  By symmetry the same argument applied to the lower‑triangular cells $(i,i-1)$, $2\\\\le i\\\\le n$, shows that at least another $n-1$ rectangles are required.  Hence any tiling uses at least $2n-2$ tiles.\\n4. Since the construction uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles, the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.  For $n=2025$ this equals $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  We are given an $n\\\\times n$ grid of unit squares.  In each row and each column exactly one unit square must remain uncovered; all other squares are to be covered by axis‑parallel rectangular tiles whose interiors are disjoint.\\n\\n#### 1. Reduction to the diagonal case\\n\\nLet $p:\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\to\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ be the permutation that records the column of the uncovered square in each row, i.e. the uncovered squares are $\\\\{(i,p(i))\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le n\\\\}$.  Permuting the columns by the bijection $p^{-1}$ sends the uncovered squares to the diagonal squares $(i,i)$.  Such a column permutation merely relabels the columns; a rectangle remains a rectangle after the relabeling, and the number of rectangles does not change.  Therefore the minimum number of tiles for an arbitrary permutation $p$ is the same as the minimum number for the identity permutation.  Hence we may assume from now on that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.\\n\\n#### 2. A tiling with $2n-2$ tiles\\n\\nConsider the upper‑triangular region\\n$$U=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i<j\\\\le n\\\\}$$\\nand the lower‑triangular region\\n$$L=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le j<i\\\\le n\\\\}.$$\\nBoth $U$ and $L$ together constitute the set of all covered squares.\\n\\nFor each column $j$ with $2\\\\le j\\\\le n$ place a vertical tile covering the cells\\n$$\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le j-1\\\\}.$$\\nThis tile has size $(j-1)\\\\times1$ and lies entirely in $U$ because every row $i$ in the tile satisfies $i<j$.\\n\\nFor each column $j$ with $1\\\\le j\\\\le n-1$ place a vertical tile covering the cells\\n$$\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid j+1\\\\le i\\\\le n\\\\}.$$\\nThis tile has size $(n-j)\\\\times1$ and lies entirely in $L$ because every row $i$ in the tile satisfies $i>j$.\\n\\nThe two families of tiles are disjoint, they cover all cells of $U$ and $L$, and none of them contains a diagonal cell.  The number of tiles is\\n$$(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.$$\\nThus $2n-2$ tiles are sufficient.\\n\\n#### 3. A lower bound of $2n-2$ tiles\\n\\nLet a tiling of the board with diagonal holes be given.  Observe the following crucial property.\\n\\n**Lemma 1.**  Every tile is either completely contained in $U$ or completely contained in $L$.\\n*Proof.*  Suppose a tile $R$ contains a cell $(i,j)$ with $i<j$ (i.e. a cell of $U$) and also a cell $(k,\\\\ell)$ with $k>\\\\ell$ (a cell of $L$).  Because $R$ is a rectangle, its row interval $[\\\\min\\\\{i,k\\\\},\\\\max\\\\{i,k\\\\}]$ and its column interval $[\\\\min\\\\{j,\\\\ell\\\\},\\\\max\\\\{j,\\\\ell\\\\}]$ are both contiguous.  Consequently the diagonal cell $(\\\\max\\\\{i,k\\\\},\\\\max\\\\{j,\\\\ell\\\\})$ lies inside $R$.  That diagonal cell is uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that tiles may not cover uncovered squares.  Hence a tile cannot intersect both $U$ and $L$.  ∎\\n\\nNow focus on $U$.  Define the set\\n$$A=\\\\{(i,i+1)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1\\\\}.$$\\nAll elements of $A$ belong to $U$.  Consider any tile $R\\\\subseteq U$.  By Lemma 1, $R$ is a rectangle whose rows form an interval $[a,b]$ and whose columns form an interval $[c,d]$ with the additional condition $b<c$ (otherwise a cell with row $\\\\ge c$ and column $\\\\le c$ would lie on or below the diagonal).  The only possible element of $A$ that can lie in $R$ is $(b,b+1)$, and this occurs only when $c=b+1$.  Hence **each tile contained in $U$ can cover at most one element of $A$**.\\n\\nSince the $n-1$ cells of $A$ must all be covered, at least $n-1$ distinct tiles are required for $U$.\\n\\nBy symmetry, the lower‑triangular region $L$ contains the set\\n$$B=\\\\{(i,i-1)\\\\mid 2\\\\le i\\\\le n\\\\},$$\\nand an analogous argument shows that each tile contained in $L$ can cover at most one element of $B$.  Consequently at least $n-1$ tiles are needed for $L$.\\n\\nBecause tiles belonging to $U$ and tiles belonging to $L$ are disjoint, the total number of tiles in any tiling is at least\\n$$(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.$$\\n∎\\n\\n#### 4. Conclusion\\n\\nWe have exhibited a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles and proved that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly $2n-2$.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ this minimum equals\\n$$2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.$$\\nThus Matilda needs at least, and can achieve, $4048$ tiles.\\n\\n**Answer:** $4048$.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \