gle region\\nWe prove that **any** permutation $\\\\pi$ forces $L$ to require at least $n-1$ rectangles.  The same argument, applied to $R$, will then give the overall lower bound $2n-2$.\\n\\n##### Lemma 1.\\nLet $L$ be defined as above.  For each row $i$ with $\\\\pi(i)>1$ let\\n\\\\[\\nr_i = (i,\\\\,\\\\pi(i)-1)\\\\]\\nbe the rightmost cell of $L$ in that row.  The $n-1$ cells $\\\\{r_i\\\\}$ are all distinct.  In any tiling of $L$ by axis‑parallel rectangles:\\n1. a rectangle can contain **at most one** of the cells $r_i$;\\n2. every rectangle contains **at least one** of the cells $r_i$.\\nConsequently at least $n-1$ rectangles are necessary.\\n\\n*Proof of Lemma 1.*\\nThe numbers $a_i:=\\\\pi(i)-1$ form a permutation of $\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$; therefore exactly one row has $a_i=0$ (the row in which $\\\\pi(i)=1$) and the remaining $n-1$ rows have $a_i\\\\ge1$.  For each such row the cell $r_i$ is the unique cell of $L$ in column $a_i$ of that row.\\n\\n1.  Suppose a rectangle $R=I\\\\times J$ (with $I$ a set of rows and $J$ a set of columns) contains two distinct row‑end cells $r_{i}$ and $r_{k}$ with $i\\\\neq k$.  Without loss of generality $a_i<a_k$.  Because $R$ contains $r_k$, its column set $J$ must contain the column $a_k$.  Since $J$ is an interval of columns, it also contains every column $c$ with $a_i<c\\\\le a_k$, in particular column $a_k$ itself.  But row $i$ has no cell of $L$ beyond column $a_i$, so the square $(i,a_k)$ is not in $L$.  Hence $R$ would contain a cell outside $L$, contradicting that $R$ is a tile of $L$.  Therefore a rectangle can contain at most one row‑end.\\n\\n2.  Let $R=I\\\\times J$ be a rectangle of a tiling of $L$.  Set $i_0$ to be a row in $I$ for which $a_{i_0}=\\\\min\\\\{a_i\\\\mid i\\\\in I\\\\}$.  Because $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,a_{i_0}\\\\}$, we may enlarge $J$ to the interval $\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,a_{i_0}\\\\}$ without intersecting any hole: all squares $(i_0,j)$ with $j\\\\le a_{i_0}$ belong to $L$, and