    "solution": "### Summary ###\n\na. Verdict: I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\nb. Method Sketch: \n   - Model the uncovered squares as a permutation matrix: there is a permutation $\\pi$ of $\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}$ (with $n=2025$) such that the unique uncovered square in row $i$ lies in column $\\pi(i)$. \n   - Split the set of covered squares into two disjoint regions: \n        $L=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\}$ (the cells strictly left of the uncovered square in each row) and \n        $R=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}$ (the cells strictly right of the uncovered square). \n   - Show an explicit construction (take $\\pi(i)=i$, the main diagonal) that tiles $L$ and $R$ with $n-1$ vertical rectangles each, giving $2n-2$ tiles. This provides an upper bound of $2n-2$. \n   - Prove a lower bound: for any permutation $\\pi$, each of $L$ and $R$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles. The proof uses the \u201crow\u2011end\u201d (resp. \u201ccolumn\u2011end\u201d) cells \u2013 the rightmost covered cell in each non\u2011empty row of $L$ (resp. the leftmost covered cell in each non\u2011empty row of $R$). These $n-1$ cells are all distinct, any rectangle can contain at most one of them, and every rectangle must contain at least one of them. Hence at least $n-1$ rectangles are required for $L$, and similarly for $R$. \n   - Combining the two bounds yields the exact minimum $2n-2$, and for $n=2025$ this equals $4048$.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet $n=2025$.  In each row of the $n\\times n$ board there is exactly one uncovered unit square, and each column also contains exactly one uncovered square.  Consequently the set $U$ of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation $\\pi$ of $\\{1,2,\\dots,n\\}$ such that\n\\[\nU=\\{\\,(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,2,\\dots,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.  Because the sides of every tile lie on grid lines and tiles are not allowed to overlap, a tile is exactly an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose cells are a Cartesian product $I\\times J$ of a set $I$ of rows and a set $J$ of columns.\n\n---\n#### 1.  Decomposition into two regions\nFor a fixed permutation $\\pi$ define\n\\[\nL = \\{\\,(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\,\\},\\qquad\nR = \\{\\,(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe sets $L$ and $R$ are disjoint and $L\\cup R$ is precisely the set of covered squares.  Geometrically $L$ consists of the cells that lie strictly to the left of the uncovered square in their row, while $R$ consists of the cells strictly to the right.\nEvery tile lies entirely in $L$ or entirely in $R$, because a rectangle that contained cells from both sides would also contain the uncovered square separating the two sides \u2013 which is forbidden.\nThus the problem separates into two independent sub\u2011problems: tile $L$ and tile $R$ with the smallest possible numbers of rectangles, then add the two numbers.\n\n---\n#### 2.  An upper bound \u2013 a construction with $2n-2$ tiles\nChoose the permutation $\\pi(i)=i$ (the main diagonal).  Then\n\\[\nL = \\{\\,(i,j)\\mid j<i\\,\\},\\qquad\nR = \\{\\,(i,j)\\mid j>i\\,\\}.\n\\]\nBoth $L$ and $R$ are staircase shapes.  Tile $L$ by the $n-1$ vertical rectangles\n\\[\nC_j = \\{\\,(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\,\\},\\qquad j=2,3,\\dots,n.\n\\]\nEach $C_j$ has height $j-1$ and width $1$, and the family $\\{C_j\\}$ partitions $L$.  Similarly tile $R$ by the $n-1$ vertical rectangles\n\\[\nD_j = \\{\\,(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\,\\},\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots,n-1.\n\\]\nThus $L$ needs $n-1$ tiles and $R$ needs $n-1$ tiles, for a total of $2n-2$ tiles.  Consequently\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ \\le\\ 2n-2.\n\\]\nFor $n=2025$ this gives the concrete upper bound $4048$.\n\n---\n#### 3.  A lower bound for a single region\nWe prove that **any** permutation $\\pi$ forces $L$ to require at least $n-1$ rectangles.  The same argument, applied to $R$, will then give the overall lower bound $2n-2$.\n\n##### Lemma 1.\nLet $L$ be defined as above.  For each row $i$ with $\\pi(i)>1$ let\n\\[\nr_i = (i,\\,\\pi(i)-1)\\]\nbe the rightmost cell of $L$ in that row.  The $n-1$ cells $\\{r_i\\}$ are all distinct.  In any tiling of $L$ by axis\u2011parallel rectangles:\n1. a rectangle can contain **at most one** of the cells $r_i$;\n2. every rectangle contains **at least one** of the cells $r_i$.\nConsequently at least $n-1$ rectangles are necessary.\n\n*Proof of Lemma 1.*\nThe numbers $a_i:=\\pi(i)-1$ form a permutation of $\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}$; therefore exactly one row has $a_i=0$ (the row in which $\\pi(i)=1$) and the remaining $n-1$ rows have $a_i\\ge1$.  For each such row the cell $r_i$ is the unique cell of $L$ in column $a_i$ of that row.\n\n1.  Suppose a rectangle $R=I\\times J$ (with $I$ a set of rows and $J$ a set of columns) contains two distinct row\u2011end cells $r_{i}$ and $r_{k}$ with $i\\neq k$.  Without loss of generality $a_i<a_k$.  Because $R$ contains $r_k$, its column set $J$ must contain the column $a_k$.  Since $J$ is an interval of columns, it also contains every column $c$ with $a_i<c\\le a_k$, in particular column $a_k$ itself.  But row $i$ has no cell of $L$ beyond column $a_i$, so the square $(i,a_k)$ is not in $L$.  Hence $R$ would contain a cell outside $L$, contradicting that $R$ is a tile of $L$.  Therefore a rectangle can contain at most one row\u2011end.\n\n2.  Let $R=I\\times J$ be a rectangle of a tiling of $L$.  Set $i_0$ to be a row in $I$ for which $a_{i_0}=\\min\\{a_i\\mid i\\in I\\}$.  Because $J\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots,a_{i_0}\\}$, we may enlarge $J$ to the interval $\\{1,2,\\dots,a_{i_0}\\}$ without intersecting any hole: all squares $(i_0,j)$ with $j\\le a_{i_0}$ belong to $L$, and for any other row $i\\in I$ we have $a_i\\ge a_{i_0}$, so the enlarged rectangle still stays inside $L$.  In a tiling that uses the smallest possible number of rectangles, such an enlargement would be impossible only if $R$ already contains the cell $(i_0,a_{i_0})=r_{i_0}$.  Hence every rectangle of a minimal tiling contains at least one row\u2011end.\n\nSince there are exactly $n-1$ distinct row\u2011end cells and each rectangle contributes at most one of them, any tiling of $L$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.  \u220e\n\n---\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the whole board\nApplying Lemma&nbsp;1 to the region $L$ yields $\\ge n-1$ tiles.  The region $R$ is the mirror image of $L$ (replace each column $j$ by $n+1-j$), so the same argument shows that $R$ also needs at least $n-1$ tiles.  Because the two regions are disjoint, the total number of tiles required to cover $L\\cup R$ is at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ \\ge\\ 2n-2.\n\\]\nCombined with the construction of Section&nbsp;2 we obtain equality:\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n---\n#### 5.  Evaluation for $n=2025$\nSubstituting $n=2025$ gives\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nHence Matilda can achieve the required condition with $4048$ tiles, and no arrangement can use fewer.\n\n**Answer:** $\\boxed{4048}$.\n",
