ed square and $r_i$ covered squares to the right; note $\\ell_i+r_i=n-1$ and the multiset $\\{\\ell_i\\}$ equals $\\{0,1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ (the same holds for $\\{r_i\\}$).  A tile is a rectangle $T=I\\times J$, where $I$ and $J$ are intervals of rows and columns respectively.\n\n**Lemma 1.**  Let $T=I\\times J$ be a tile.  Then either $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,\\ell_i\\}$ for every $i\\in I$ (a *left\u2011type* tile) or $J\\subseteq\\{p(i)+1,\\dots,n\\}$ for every $i\\in I$ (a *right\u2011type* tile).\n\n*Proof.*  If $T$ contains a row $i\\in I$, it cannot contain column $p(i)$ because $(i,p(i))$ is uncovered.  Hence $J$ must lie entirely to the left of $p(i)$ (i.e. $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,\\ell_i\\}$) or entirely to the right of $p(i)$ (i.e. $J\\subseteq\\{p(i)+1,\\dots,n\\}$).  Since $J$ is the same for all rows of $I$, the same alternative must hold for every $i\\in I$, giving the two possibilities.  \u220e\n\n**Lemma 2.**  If $T$ is a left\u2011type tile covering rows $I$ and columns $\\{1,\\dots,c\\}$, then $c=\\min_{i\\in I}\\ell_i$, and consequently $c=\\ell_{i_0}$ for the unique row $i_0\\in I$ where the minimum is attained.\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma\u00a01 we have $c\\le \\ell_i$ for all $i\\in I$.  If $c$ were strictly smaller than $\\min_{i\\in I}\\ell_i$, we could enlarge $J$ by one column and still stay left of every hole, contradicting maximality of $J$ in the definition of a tile.  Hence $c=\\min_{i\\in I}\\ell_i$, and because the $\\ell_i$ are distinct this minimum occurs at a unique row $i_0$, giving $c=\\ell_{i_0}$.  \u220e\n\n**Corollary 1.**  Every left\u2011type tile has a width equal to $\\ell_i$ for some row $i$, and that row cannot belong to any left\u2011type tile of larger width.  Since the values $\\ell_i$ run through $0,1,\\dots,n-1$, each positive integer $k\\in\\{1,\\dots,n-1\\}$ must appear as the width of at least one left\u2011type tile.  Therefore any tiling uses at least $n-1$ left\u2011type tiles.\n\nThe same reasoning applied to the right side (using the numbers $r_i=n-p(i)$) yields:\n\n**Corollary 2.**  Any tiling uses at least $n-1$ right\u2011type tiles.\n\nCombining the two corollaries we obtain the lower bound\n\\[\\text{#tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\tag{1}\\]\n\n**Construction attaining the bound.**  Take the identity permutation $p(i)=i$ for $i=1,\\dots,n$.  The uncovered squares are the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.  For each $i=2,\\dots,n$ place a left\u2011type tile\n\\[T_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots,i-1\\},\\]\nwhich has width $\\ell_i=i-1$.  For each $i=1,\\dots,n-1$ place a right\u2011type tile\n\\[T_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots,n\\},\\]\nwhich has width $r_i=n-i$.  The $2n-2$ tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every unit square except the $n$ diagonal squares, exactly as required.  Hence the lower bound (1) is tight.\n\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\n\\[\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\]\n\nThus the answer is $\\boxed{4048}$.\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\nVerify that:\n1. Lemma\u00a01 correctly classifies every possible tile as left\u2011type or right\u2011type.\n2. Lemma\u00a02 and its corollaries rigorously show that each positive $\\ell_i$ (resp. $r_i$) forces a distinct tile, giving the lower bound $2n-2$.\n3. The construction for the identity permutation indeed uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles, covers all squares except the diagonal, and contains no overlaps.\n4. The final numeric computation $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$ is correct.\nAll statements are justified and no step relies on unproven intuition.",
    "final_answer": "4048"
}
