ery square except the $n$ diagonal squares.\\n- For $n=2025$ the minimal number of tiles is $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$.\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  Choose a permutation $p$ of $\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  The uncovered unit squares are\\n\\\\[U=\\\\{(i,p(i))\\\\mid i=1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nFor each row $i$ set\\n\\\\[\\\\ell_i:=p(i)-1,\\\\qquad r_i:=n-p(i).\\n\\\\]Thus row $i$ contains $\\\\ell_i$ covered squares to the left of the uncovered square and $r_i$ covered squares to the right; clearly $\\\\ell_i+r_i=n-1$.\\nBecause $p$ is a permutation, the numbers $\\\\ell_i$ form the set $\\\\{0,1,\\\\dots,n-1\\\\}$ (each value occurs exactly once).  The same holds for $r_i$.\\n\\nA tile is a rectangle $T=I\\\\times J$, where $I$ and $J$ are non‑empty intervals of rows and columns respectively.  Since a tile may not contain an uncovered square, for every $i\\\\in I$ the column interval $J$ must avoid column $p(i)$.  Consequently, for each $i\\\\in I$ either $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,\\\\ell_i\\\\}$ (the whole tile lies to the left of the hole in that row) or $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{p(i)+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ (the whole tile lies to the right).  Because $J$ is the same for all rows of $I$, the same alternative must hold for every $i\\\\in I$.  Hence each tile is of exactly one of the following two types:\\n\\n* **Left‑type:** $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,\\\\ell_i\\\\}$ for all $i\\\\in I$.\\n* **Right‑type:** $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{p(i)+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ for all $i\\\\in I$.\\n\\nWe treat the two types separately.\\n\\n#### Left‑type tiles\\nFor a left‑type tile $T=I\\\\times J$, let $j_{\\\\max}=\\\\max J$.  Since $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{1,\\\\dots,\\\\ell_i\\\\}$ for each $i\\\\in I$, we have\\n\\\\[j_{\\\\max}\\\\le \\\\min_{i\\\\in I}\\\\ell_i.\\\\tag{1}\\\\]\\nConsider the *rightmost covered cell* of row $i$, namely $(i,\\\\ell_i)$ (this cell exists exactly when $\\\\ell_i>0$).  Because $\\\\ell_i$ is the largest column belonging to the left region of row $i$, the cell $(i,\\\\ell_i)$ lies in column $j_{\\\\max}$ if and only if $\\\\ell_i=j_{\\\\max}$.  By (1) the inequality $\\\\ell_i\\\\ge j_{\\\\max}$ holds for every $i\\\\in I$, and equality can hold for at most one $i$, because the numbers $\\\\ell_i$ are all distinct.  Therefore a left‑type tile can contain the rightmost covered cell of at most one row.\\n\\nSince there are exactly $n-1$ rows with $\\\\ell_i>0$, the $n-1$ cells $(i,\\\\ell_i)$ (for those rows) must each be covered by some left‑type tile, and each left‑type tile can cover at most one of them.  Consequently any tiling requires at least $n-1$ left‑type tiles.\\n\\n#### Right‑type tiles\\nFor a right‑type tile $T=I\\\\times J$, let $j_{\\\\min}=\\\\min J$.  The condition $J\\\\subseteq\\\\{p(i)+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ for all $i\\\\in I$ implies\\n\\\\[j_{\\\\min}\\\\ge \\\\max_{i\\\\in I}p(i)+1 = \\\\max_{i\\\\in I}(\\\\ell_i+1)+1 = \\\\max_{i\\\\in I}\\\\ell_i+2.\\\\]\\nMore conveniently, define for each row $i$ the *leftmost covered cell* of the right region as $(i,p(i)+1)$.  Its column index is $p(i)+1=\\\\ell_i+2$.  By the same reasoning as above, a right‑type tile can contain at most one of these cells, because the column indices $p(i)+1$ are all distinct.  There are $n-1$ rows with $r_i>0$, i.e. $n-1$ such cells, so at least $n-1$ right‑type tiles are necessary.\\n\\n#### Lower bound\\nAdding the two lower bounds we obtain\\n\\\\[\\\\text{#tiles}\\\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\\\tag{2}\\\\]\\nThus any admissible placement uses at least $2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\n#### Construction attaining the bound\\nTake the identity permutation $p(i)=i$ for $i=1,\\\\dots,n$.  The uncovered squares are the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.  For each $i=2,\\\\dots,n$ place a left‑type tile\\n\\\\[T_i^{\\\\text{L}}=\\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,i-1\\\\},\\\\]\\nwhich covers exactly the $i-1$ squares to the left of the hole in row $i$.  For each $i=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ place a right‑type tile\\n\\\\[T_i^{\\\\text{R}}=\\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{i+1,i+2,\\\\dots,n\\\\},\\\\]\\nwhich covers the $n-i$ squares to the right of the hole in row $i$.  The $2n-2$ tiles are pairwise disjoint, they cover every square of the board except the $n$ diagonal squares, and each row and each column indeed has exactly one uncovered square.\\n\\nTherefore the lower bound (2) is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals $2n-2$.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the required minimum number of tiles is $\\\\boxed{4048}$.\\n\\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\\nVerify that:\\n1. Lemma 1 correctly classifies every tile as left‑type or right‑type.\\n2. The argument that a left‑type tile can contain at most one cell $(i,\\\\ell_i)$ uses the distinctness of the $\\\\ell_i$ and the inequality (1); the same reasoning applies to right‑type tiles.\\n3. The counting of rows with $\\\\ell_i>0$ (resp. $r_i>0$) yields exactly $n-1$ required cells for each side, establishing the lower bound $2n-2$.\\n4. The construction for the identity permutation indeed uses $2n-2$ tiles, covers all squares except the diagonal, and respects the condition that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square.\\n5. The final arithmetic $2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048$ is correct.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
